1
00:00:01,460 --> 00:00:03,740
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:04,960
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,500
My name is Catherine.

4
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:10,010
And today we have a very advanced lesson for you all about medicine.

5
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:10,870
That's right.

6
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:16,450
Today we're going to take a look at what is very widely known as Chinese traditional medicine.

7
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,670
So a very interesting topic in general.

8
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:20,450
That's right.

9
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:25,370
And for people who use this, they might call it TCM, traditional Chinese medicine.

10
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:27,770
So remember that there's that acronym as well.

11
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:34,330
But let's take a listen to today's dialogue and when we come back, we'll be discussing some of the important words and phrases in the dialogue.

12
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,200
What's wrong?

13
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:42,430
I have a headache.

14
00:00:43,410 --> 00:00:45,810
These past few days I've been living off painkillers.

15
00:00:46,410 --> 00:00:48,350
Man, I feel like my head is going to explode.

16
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:50,350
You should get acupuncture treatment.

17
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:53,710
My mom was always having headache issues and it was acupuncture that cured her.

18
00:00:54,070 --> 00:00:55,290
The results are too slow.

19
00:00:56,030 --> 00:01:00,690
On top of that, just the thought of smoking needles poking into my flesh frightens me.

20
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:02,810
They don't just randomly stick you.

21
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:04,030
They find your pressure points.

22
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:09,440
The heat allows the body to immediately respond to the treatment, restoring the body's qi.

23
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:11,590
But I get scared the moment I see a needle.

24
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:15,190
How could I stand having needles in my body for hours on end?

25
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:16,950
The needles are very thin.

26
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:21,470
And as long as the doctor's technique is good and the patient himself is relaxed, it won't hurt.

27
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:24,290
On the contrary, it will actually alleviate pain.

28
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,200
Now there are high-tech needles that are micro-thin.

29
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:27,830
They don't hurt at all.

30
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:31,890
However, if you are really scared of acupuncture, scraping or cupping are also options.

31
00:01:32,310 --> 00:01:33,670
Scraping is too terrifying.

32
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:37,690
When they finish scraping, your body is all red as if you were just tortured.

33
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Cupping is the same.

34
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,830
Your body ends up with red circles all over it.

35
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:43,090
It looks like someone beat you up.

36
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:46,230
This only signifies that the toxins have left the body.

37
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:48,810
Actually, there's only discomfort during the treatment process.

38
00:01:49,170 --> 00:01:50,870
Once it's over, you'll feel very comfortable.

39
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:52,670
Chinese medicine is strange.

40
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:56,230
The patients are already ill and then the doctor makes them suffer more.

41
00:01:56,810 --> 00:01:58,390
This is the only way to get at the problem.

42
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:02,130
Anyway, if you want to relieve the pain, you are just going to have to be tough and do it.

43
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:02,910
Forget it.

44
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:05,370
I don't want to inflict any more pain on myself.

45
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:08,950
In a little while, I'll go and buy some more painkillers and take a nap.

46
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:13,040
All right, we're back.

47
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:29,040
So now obviously we are talking about what is commonly known as alternative medicine because we know that traditional medicine nowadays is doctors, you have antibiotics, that sort of thing.

48
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:34,880
And here you're actually using these techniques and these different ways of curing someone.

49
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:37,920
So the first thing that comes to mind here is acupuncture.

50
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:39,240
That's right.

51
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:50,550
So acupuncture is usually considered a part of Chinese medicine and it's a kind of medicine that uses needles in different parts of the body.

52
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:57,130
So it might look really scary because you see people lying on their stomachs with needles coming out of their backs.

53
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:03,280
And the places where they put the needles in, they're called pressure points, right?

54
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:03,460
That's right.

55
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:07,460
And pressure points are things that we talk about with massages as well.

56
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:15,520
So a good masseuse, a person who does a massage, will also look for these pressure points, which are key points on the body.

57
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:23,940
And in acupuncture, they'll put these needles into those points as well because each point relates to a different part of the body, like the liver or the kidneys.

58
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:24,860
Right.

59
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:37,800
So it's actually very interesting, the fact that by sticking a needle in one of these pressure points, they are curing or alleviating the pain of maybe, like you say, your kidney or your liver or something like this.

60
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:42,640
This is a really great word though, Marco, the word that you just used, to alleviate pain.

61
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:46,840
Now to alleviate pain means to make the pain go away.

62
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:53,020
So we heard the opposite of that as well, also at the very end of today's dialogue.

63
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:56,160
So the opposite of alleviate would be to inflict pain.

64
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,020
This is to do pain to yourself or to someone else.

65
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:01,580
To cause pain.

66
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:02,580
To cause pain.

67
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:06,820
So to alleviate is to take it away and to inflict is to cause.

68
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:10,800
So now acupuncture is very popular.

69
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:19,680
Sticking needles in people's pressure points, it does seem like it would hurt, but there are other ones that maybe are not as widely known.

70
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:23,440
The next one that we're going to talk about is scraping.

71
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:24,300
Scraping.

72
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:37,130
So to scrape is a verb and you can scrape the bottom of your plate with your spoon or you can scrape on, you know, sometimes cats like to scrape on the furniture.

73
00:04:37,570 --> 00:04:40,440
They use their claws and make that sound.

74
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,320
But in this case, scraping is a little bit more maybe painful sounding.

75
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:47,420
What do they do in scraping?

76
00:04:47,420 --> 00:04:48,940
So basically it's the same thing.

77
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:54,080
They use different tools and they scrape your body.

78
00:04:54,080 --> 00:05:05,160
They just, I don't know, basically what I understand from it is that they are extracting the illness by taking it out of your skin.

79
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:10,600
So they use what looks like a small knife or like a flat kind of sharp blade.

80
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,060
And they actually kind of take off the top layer of your skin.

81
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:15,240
They scrape it away.

82
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:19,180
It's very, very painful from what I've heard.

83
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:35,540
I've never done it myself, but as we see in the dialogue, it does look very, it looks like you've been hit or treated poorly at the prison or something because your skin is red and almost bleeding.

84
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:36,600
That's right.

85
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:41,730
And another one that actually looks quite painful when you see someone after they've done it is called cupping.

86
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:42,350
Right.

87
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:47,590
Now, this one you might have seen even in the last Karate Kid movie.

88
00:05:48,520 --> 00:06:13,330
Basically it's a little round bulb or a little round glass cup and the doctor, the Chinese doctor will light a piece of cotton and will put the cup over it and the suction effect that by putting out the fire and it sucks up all the oxygen and everything causes the cup to suck onto your skin really, really tight.

89
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:18,520
You put it on your skin so it creates a vacuum, right?

90
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:19,520
Exactly.

91
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:20,110
There's no air coming in.

92
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:31,790
And that way the cup that there's no air in has to pull out all those chemicals and those toxins and that oil from your skin and take it away.

93
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:33,400
Right.

94
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:34,400
Right.

95
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:35,400
So it's actually pulling at your skin.

96
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:36,400
Yeah.

97
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:54,400
And well, that's the thing with the vacuum effect is so great and pulling it out with such force that you're actually left with a big purple or red circle on your skin from I guess the blood clot type thing that is caused by it.

98
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:58,400
So you will see a person's back maybe and they have bruises.

99
00:06:58,400 --> 00:06:58,520
Bruises.

100
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:02,240
Yeah, like six, seven, ten bruises on their backs.

101
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:09,740
Although I am told that even though it's very painful and it looks even more painful, the effect is that it does help your circulation.

102
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:13,460
It helps the blood in your body move around more freely.

103
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:15,280
Yeah, I've heard the same thing.

104
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:27,960
And even though that it looks painful and it may be painful while they're doing it, I've heard that after it's done, even though it looks like it should hurt, it looks like a bruise, it doesn't hurt like a bruise.

105
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:31,440
So people don't actually say, oh, you know, don't touch my back.

106
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:31,840
It really hurts.

107
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:33,100
It doesn't really seem that way.

108
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:37,170
Well, and there's an interesting line in the dialogue that is related to this.

109
00:07:37,730 --> 00:07:41,170
It's about scraping actually, but you can say it about cupping as well.

110
00:07:41,170 --> 00:07:45,570
Your body is all red as if you were just tortured.

111
00:07:46,450 --> 00:07:51,110
Now as if you were tortured or we could say as if you were beaten up.

112
00:07:51,970 --> 00:07:54,150
And in this case, was the person tortured?

113
00:07:54,710 --> 00:07:55,070
No.

114
00:07:55,710 --> 00:08:07,060
It's basically a relation or making that relation that your skin is so, let's say bloody that it looks as if you were tortured.

115
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:10,080
So it seems like somebody hit you with a whip or something.

116
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:12,380
It looks like you were tortured, but you weren't.

117
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:16,140
So as if is a way for us to make a comparison or an analogy.

118
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:17,720
Right.

119
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:26,600
I think you can see it in maybe books, you know, so maybe you would describe a guy saying, oh, he was really angry as if he had rabies.

120
00:08:26,750 --> 00:08:32,130
Yeah, it's as if he had rabies like he doesn't, but he's acting like he does.

121
00:08:32,290 --> 00:08:37,790
Or maybe you run into the kitchen and eat so much food as if you hadn't eaten in days.

122
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:38,390
Exactly.

123
00:08:39,010 --> 00:08:42,490
So you have obviously, but you're acting like someone who is starving.

124
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:50,010
So yeah, that is an interesting way to describe or to make a connection between two different things.

125
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:58,410
And well, Chinese traditional medicine as well, a very interesting and historical form of treating illnesses.

126
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:03,250
Now I'm told that it does work, even though some people are very skeptical about it.

127
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:07,770
And I don't think it's accepted in the international medical field.

128
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:11,090
Well, I think that there's a Western medicine which has its benefits.

129
00:09:11,810 --> 00:09:18,590
You know, you have penicillin and all of these different antibiotics to help us combat different diseases and bacteria.

130
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:23,360
But also traditional Chinese medicine kind of helps other things.

131
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:40,120
I think there is a spirituality, this idea that we have a chi, there are positive and negative forces in our bodies, and sometimes trauma or big events will make that chi kind of, well, it'll affect it in a bad way.

132
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:42,240
So the chi is unbalanced.

133
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:46,480
And so a lot of this is about rebalancing your system.

134
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:54,570
And if you believe that and you really understand that there are certain forces inside your body, then I'm sure this kind of medicine has a positive effect on you.

135
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,320
Right.

136
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:59,590
I believe that it does have its ups and downs.

137
00:10:00,290 --> 00:10:13,810
I was very skeptical about it until once, actually, I had a very, very bad headache and somebody took and pushed a pressure point on my finger, on my thumb for about maybe one or two minutes and the headache was gone.

138
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:17,720
So I was very much amazed.

139
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:32,440
But at the same time, when people are treated maybe for, you know, maybe they can conceive a baby and they're doing these rituals or giving them ginger or different roots to drink and eat and stuff.

140
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:38,440
I don't know if that really does help or what evidence there is that it actually does work.

141
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:44,100
Well, I think that's something that also is important to look at on an individual basis.

142
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:54,760
I don't think even some Western medicines help all the time if you're not in a place where you believe that it will work.

143
00:10:54,760 --> 00:11:10,800
But the interesting aspect of it is, of course, so many years ago, we're talking about hundreds or thousands of years ago, when there was no Western medicine and then people were being cured and treated by these techniques, which actually worked.

144
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:17,830
So there is a very interesting aspect that maybe even Western medicine can learn from traditional medicine like this.

145
00:11:17,990 --> 00:11:18,250
Definitely.

146
00:11:18,990 --> 00:11:25,230
So it's an interesting topic, Oriental medicine, Western medicine, but we want to know what you think about it.

147
00:11:25,530 --> 00:11:32,810
We also want to know in your home country, do you have a traditional kind of medicine or any medical alternatives to Western medicine?

148
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:35,600
Let us know, our website is EnglishPod.com.

149
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:36,910
All right, we'll see you guys there.

150
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:37,350
Bye.
